1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,839
Meet Kevin. Dave's nephew.

2
00:00:01,839 --> 00:00:03,042
Twelve years old.

3
00:00:03,042 --> 00:00:05,731
Kevin has opened a lemonade stand, and

4
00:00:05,731 --> 00:00:06,722
he has a sign.

5
00:00:07,002 --> 00:00:09,695
The sign says: voted best lemonade in

6
00:00:09,695 --> 00:00:12,316
town. Ninety-seven percent approval.

7
00:00:12,596 --> 00:00:13,901
Dave is impressed.

8
00:00:13,901 --> 00:00:15,424
Dave buys three cups.

9
00:00:15,424 --> 00:00:18,325
Then Dave asks a dangerous question: who

10
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:18,760
voted?

11
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,282
Kevin says: I did.

12
00:00:20,282 --> 00:00:23,251
Ninety-seven times. The other three times I

13
00:00:23,251 --> 00:00:24,080
was thirsty.

14
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:26,351
Dave laughs. Kevin is twelve.

15
00:00:26,351 --> 00:00:28,755
Then Dave opens the news and learns

16
00:00:28,755 --> 00:00:31,845
that a grown-up polling company did basically

17
00:00:31,845 --> 00:00:32,876
the same thing.

18
00:00:33,156 --> 00:00:35,215
Except its numbers ended up in the

19
00:00:35,215 --> 00:00:36,124
New York Times.

20
00:00:36,404 --> 00:00:38,644
Today: how did a fake pollster fool

21
00:00:38,644 --> 00:00:41,460
the political world before the midterms, who

22
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:43,188
did it hurt, and how do you

23
00:00:43,188 --> 00:00:45,108
tell a real poll from a Kevin?

24
00:00:45,388 --> 00:00:47,921
The company was called Wedgewood Polls.

25
00:00:47,921 --> 00:00:50,518
Since the spring, it had been publishing

26
00:00:50,518 --> 00:00:52,791
surveys about the big races in this

27
00:00:52,791 --> 00:00:54,415
year's midterm elections.

28
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:56,815
Senate races. Governor races.

29
00:00:56,815 --> 00:00:58,936
Swing states and safe states.

30
00:00:58,936 --> 00:01:00,910
It looked busy and serious.

31
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:04,429
Then, on Tuesday, October twentieth, it posted

32
00:01:04,429 --> 00:01:07,175
a statement. Almost all of its election

33
00:01:07,175 --> 00:01:09,568
surveys, it said, were fabricated.

34
00:01:09,848 --> 00:01:11,616
Fabricated means made up.

35
00:01:11,616 --> 00:01:12,817
Not a bad sample.

36
00:01:12,817 --> 00:01:14,090
Not a wrong guess.

37
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:16,776
Invented. Like Kevin, but with a logo.

38
00:01:17,056 --> 00:01:20,192
Wedgewood called it an experimental project to

39
00:01:20,192 --> 00:01:23,737
show, quote, the vulnerabilities, lack of oversight,

40
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:26,123
and overall low barrier to entry in

41
00:01:26,123 --> 00:01:27,555
the polling industry.

42
00:01:27,835 --> 00:01:30,175
It said the project was never intended

43
00:01:30,175 --> 00:01:31,407
to sway an election.

44
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:33,132
It called it a wake-up call.

45
00:01:33,412 --> 00:01:36,256
It described itself as a fictional entity

46
00:01:36,256 --> 00:01:38,476
run by a small group of industry

47
00:01:38,476 --> 00:01:41,252
veterans. A pretend company, run by real

48
00:01:41,252 --> 00:01:41,737
people.

49
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:45,446
Their conclusion, as reported by several outlets:

50
00:01:45,446 --> 00:01:48,314
getting taken seriously was far too easy.

51
00:01:48,594 --> 00:01:50,804
The firm's account on X has since

52
00:01:50,804 --> 00:01:53,817
disappeared. TIME magazine tried emailing it.

53
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:55,022
The email bounced.

54
00:01:55,302 --> 00:01:57,391
These polls didn't stay in a corner

55
00:01:57,391 --> 00:01:58,346
of the internet.

56
00:01:58,346 --> 00:01:59,241
They travelled.

57
00:01:59,521 --> 00:02:01,951
According to TIME, the New York Times

58
00:02:01,951 --> 00:02:05,103
cited about fifteen Wedgewood polls between June

59
00:02:05,103 --> 00:02:07,664
and October. It has since removed them.

60
00:02:07,944 --> 00:02:11,776
Decision Desk HQ, which runs election forecasts,

61
00:02:11,776 --> 00:02:15,609
removed seventeen. Newsweek, Politico and MS NOW

62
00:02:15,609 --> 00:02:18,483
had also reported Wedgewood numbers.

63
00:02:18,763 --> 00:02:22,279
Poll averages and forecast sites had pulled

64
00:02:22,279 --> 00:02:26,123
them in too, including FiftyPlusOne, the Silver

65
00:02:26,123 --> 00:02:28,658
Bulletin, VoteHub and 270toWin.

66
00:02:28,938 --> 00:02:31,076
So what did the fake polls say?

67
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:34,387
Many showed Democrats doing surprisingly well in

68
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:37,766
Republican-held states, according to Fox News and

69
00:02:37,766 --> 00:02:38,248
others.

70
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:42,227
In Mississippi, Wedgewood had Republican Senator Cindy

71
00:02:42,227 --> 00:02:45,240
Hyde-Smith up only fifty-two to forty-eight.

72
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,321
Trump carried the state comfortably in twenty

73
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:49,143
twenty-four.

74
00:02:49,423 --> 00:02:52,520
The Democrat's campaign promoted that poll, and

75
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:54,101
Newsweek wrote about it.

76
00:02:54,101 --> 00:02:55,880
Remember: invented numbers.

77
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,394
In Michigan's Senate race this month: Democrat

78
00:02:59,394 --> 00:03:02,698
Abdul El-Sayed ahead of Republican Mike Rogers,

79
00:03:02,698 --> 00:03:04,597
fifty-three to forty-seven.

80
00:03:04,597 --> 00:03:05,651
Also fictional.

81
00:03:05,931 --> 00:03:08,573
In Texas, a July Wedgewood poll had

82
00:03:08,573 --> 00:03:12,196
Democrat James Talarico leading Ken Paxton among

83
00:03:12,196 --> 00:03:15,064
Hispanic voters, sixty-four to thirty.

84
00:03:15,344 --> 00:03:19,365
Other invented surveys made Democrats look competitive

85
00:03:19,365 --> 00:03:20,780
in Kansas and Ohio.

86
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:23,982
The only real survey, according to reports,

87
00:03:23,982 --> 00:03:25,173
was in Nebraska.

88
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:27,758
And the firm asked the public for

89
00:03:27,758 --> 00:03:30,693
money. A GoFundMe for its Nebraska polling

90
00:03:30,693 --> 00:03:34,675
raised five hundred dollars toward a five-thousand-dollar

91
00:03:34,675 --> 00:03:35,024
goal.

92
00:03:35,304 --> 00:03:38,676
Dave once donated to Kevin's lemonade expansion

93
00:03:38,676 --> 00:03:40,972
fund. Dave would now like a word

94
00:03:40,972 --> 00:03:41,761
with Kevin.

95
00:03:42,041 --> 00:03:43,476
So who caught them?

96
00:03:43,476 --> 00:03:46,948
An election data site called FiftyPlusOne, run

97
00:03:46,948 --> 00:03:47,930
by analyst G.

98
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:49,062
Elliott Morris.

99
00:03:49,342 --> 00:03:52,931
At first, FiftyPlusOne says, nothing looked unusual.

100
00:03:52,931 --> 00:03:55,277
It asked Wedgewood who ran it, and

101
00:03:55,277 --> 00:03:57,900
got two names and a methods statement.

102
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:01,386
Pollsters leaving big firms and starting their

103
00:04:01,386 --> 00:04:04,732
own, sometimes paying for surveys themselves, is

104
00:04:04,732 --> 00:04:06,475
normal. So it was let in.

105
00:04:06,755 --> 00:04:09,918
On October fifteenth, Wedgewood handed over the

106
00:04:09,918 --> 00:04:12,138
raw data from a survey, which one

107
00:04:12,138 --> 00:04:15,099
report says was the Mississippi Senate poll.

108
00:04:15,379 --> 00:04:17,548
Raw data is the list behind the

109
00:04:17,548 --> 00:04:21,117
headline: every person who supposedly answered, one

110
00:04:21,117 --> 00:04:21,747
row each.

111
00:04:22,027 --> 00:04:25,076
According to one account of the investigation,

112
00:04:25,076 --> 00:04:27,993
the respondent ID codes were checked against

113
00:04:27,993 --> 00:04:31,174
real records from Pollfish, the survey platform.

114
00:04:31,174 --> 00:04:32,235
They didn't fit.

115
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:34,316
In other words, the people in the

116
00:04:34,316 --> 00:04:36,717
spreadsheet looked like people who had never

117
00:04:36,717 --> 00:04:37,154
existed.

118
00:04:37,434 --> 00:04:41,082
On October twentieth, FiftyPlusOne asked Wedgewood to

119
00:04:41,082 --> 00:04:42,871
comment before publishing.

120
00:04:42,871 --> 00:04:45,417
Wedgewood didn't answer the question.

121
00:04:45,417 --> 00:04:47,689
It posted its confession instead.

122
00:04:47,969 --> 00:04:50,514
And it accused the aggregators of not

123
00:04:50,514 --> 00:04:53,815
vetting it. FiftyPlusOne calls that claim false.

124
00:04:54,095 --> 00:04:57,304
Morris wrote that the social experiment story

125
00:04:57,304 --> 00:04:58,873
was a cover for fraud.

126
00:04:58,873 --> 00:05:01,940
Former Obama staffer Tommy Vietor called it

127
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:03,081
lying and fraud.

128
00:05:03,361 --> 00:05:05,107
Now, did any of this matter?

129
00:05:05,107 --> 00:05:07,414
One candidate says it mattered a lot.

130
00:05:07,694 --> 00:05:11,100
Michigan state senator Mallory McMorrow was running

131
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,637
in the Democratic primary for the U.S.

132
00:05:13,637 --> 00:05:14,105
Senate.

133
00:05:14,385 --> 00:05:17,185
On June twenty-ninth, a Wedgewood poll gave

134
00:05:17,185 --> 00:05:19,854
her nine percent among registered voters.

135
00:05:20,134 --> 00:05:22,318
To be fair to the numbers: real

136
00:05:22,318 --> 00:05:24,783
polls from other firms also had her

137
00:05:24,783 --> 00:05:27,531
in single digits around then, with lots

138
00:05:27,531 --> 00:05:28,940
of voters undecided.

139
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,135
Still, McMorrow says that after the Wedgewood

140
00:05:32,135 --> 00:05:35,245
numbers spread, donations dried up and reporters

141
00:05:35,245 --> 00:05:37,059
kept asking when she'd quit.

142
00:05:37,339 --> 00:05:40,056
She suspended her campaign on July fifth,

143
00:05:40,056 --> 00:05:42,243
about a month before the primary.

144
00:05:42,523 --> 00:05:44,776
A strategist who ran a group backing

145
00:05:44,776 --> 00:05:47,029
her says donors pointed to that poll

146
00:05:47,029 --> 00:05:49,344
to pull money, while the campaign was

147
00:05:49,344 --> 00:05:51,535
outspent by eighty million dollars.

148
00:05:51,815 --> 00:05:54,249
McMorrow wrote that the whole thing was

149
00:05:54,249 --> 00:05:56,433
treated like a game, and called for

150
00:05:56,433 --> 00:05:58,867
a big shake-up of campaign money rules.

151
00:05:59,147 --> 00:06:01,955
And Wedgewood wasn't the first this year.

152
00:06:01,955 --> 00:06:04,832
In August, a firm called Median Strategies

153
00:06:04,832 --> 00:06:07,640
admitted its polls were fake too, calling

154
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:09,352
them a social experiment.

155
00:06:09,632 --> 00:06:13,907
Median claimed one candidate led Wisconsin's Democratic

156
00:06:13,907 --> 00:06:16,550
governor primary by twenty points.

157
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:19,581
Her rival actually won, by a razor-thin

158
00:06:19,581 --> 00:06:20,125
margin.

159
00:06:20,405 --> 00:06:23,273
It also claimed Los Angeles mayor Karen

160
00:06:23,273 --> 00:06:25,920
Bass led by twelve points, from five

161
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,685
hundred sixty residents.

162
00:06:27,685 --> 00:06:29,891
In the real primary she led by

163
00:06:29,891 --> 00:06:30,259
five.

164
00:06:30,539 --> 00:06:32,099
So how is it this easy?

165
00:06:32,099 --> 00:06:34,541
First, quick lesson: how a real poll

166
00:06:34,541 --> 00:06:34,948
works.

167
00:06:35,228 --> 00:06:36,967
You can't ask every voter.

168
00:06:36,967 --> 00:06:39,242
So you ask a sample, maybe several

169
00:06:39,242 --> 00:06:41,851
hundred to a thousand people, chosen to

170
00:06:41,851 --> 00:06:43,590
look like the whole state.

171
00:06:43,870 --> 00:06:46,104
Then you add a margin of error,

172
00:06:46,104 --> 00:06:47,256
the wiggle room.

173
00:06:47,256 --> 00:06:49,850
Median's fake LA poll even came with

174
00:06:49,850 --> 00:06:51,868
one: four point one percent.

175
00:06:51,868 --> 00:06:52,877
Very official.

176
00:06:53,157 --> 00:06:54,460
Here's the problem.

177
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:56,450
A finished poll is just a few

178
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:58,165
numbers and a nice chart.

179
00:06:58,165 --> 00:07:00,292
Anyone with a laptop can make a

180
00:07:00,292 --> 00:07:01,047
nice chart.

181
00:07:01,327 --> 00:07:04,555
Aggregators can check methods and track records,

182
00:07:04,555 --> 00:07:06,371
but a brand new firm has no

183
00:07:06,371 --> 00:07:08,860
record yet. They mostly have to trust

184
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:10,070
what they're told.

185
00:07:10,350 --> 00:07:12,378
And polls aren't just scores.

186
00:07:12,378 --> 00:07:15,035
Researchers say they change behaviour.

187
00:07:15,035 --> 00:07:18,532
A twenty twenty-three study found polls demobilize

188
00:07:18,532 --> 00:07:21,259
the minority and mobilize the majority.

189
00:07:21,539 --> 00:07:23,839
Translation: if you think your side is

190
00:07:23,839 --> 00:07:25,534
losing, you might stay home.

191
00:07:25,534 --> 00:07:27,652
If you think it's winning, you show

192
00:07:27,652 --> 00:07:27,834
up.

193
00:07:28,114 --> 00:07:31,412
A nineteen ninety-six experiment found people shown

194
00:07:31,412 --> 00:07:33,741
a made-up poll before a primary were

195
00:07:33,741 --> 00:07:36,069
more likely to back the candidate it

196
00:07:36,069 --> 00:07:36,651
favoured.

197
00:07:36,931 --> 00:07:38,489
Donors read polls too.

198
00:07:38,489 --> 00:07:40,754
Reporters build stories on them.

199
00:07:40,754 --> 00:07:43,161
One bad number can decide who gets

200
00:07:43,161 --> 00:07:46,346
money, airtime, and the dreaded question: are

201
00:07:46,346 --> 00:07:47,550
you dropping out?

202
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:49,681
So what are officials saying?

203
00:07:49,681 --> 00:07:51,915
This one, at least, upset people in

204
00:07:51,915 --> 00:07:54,532
both parties, though not always about the

205
00:07:54,532 --> 00:07:55,234
same thing.

206
00:07:55,514 --> 00:07:58,631
Notice the pattern: many fake polls flattered

207
00:07:58,631 --> 00:08:01,194
Democrats in red states, but the most

208
00:08:01,194 --> 00:08:03,688
damaging one, by her account, hurt a

209
00:08:03,688 --> 00:08:04,311
Democrat.

210
00:08:04,591 --> 00:08:08,128
The chair of the Federal Communications Commission,

211
00:08:08,128 --> 00:08:11,527
Republican Brendan Carr, pointed to a significant

212
00:08:11,527 --> 00:08:14,440
surge in fake polls, without naming cases.

213
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,381
He blamed news outlets for airing them

214
00:08:17,381 --> 00:08:20,603
without regard, he said, to basic journalistic

215
00:08:20,603 --> 00:08:21,093
ethics.

216
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:25,134
Democratic commissioner Anna Gomez answered: pollsters who

217
00:08:25,134 --> 00:08:28,376
intentionally mislead the public should answer for

218
00:08:28,376 --> 00:08:28,571
it.

219
00:08:28,851 --> 00:08:31,594
But she added that broadcasters reporting polls

220
00:08:31,594 --> 00:08:33,928
in good faith shouldn't be punished when

221
00:08:33,928 --> 00:08:35,271
a poll turns out wrong.

222
00:08:35,551 --> 00:08:38,633
Democratic strategist Mike Nellis told Fox News

223
00:08:38,633 --> 00:08:41,781
the people responsible should be prosecuted, and

224
00:08:41,781 --> 00:08:44,536
asked whether donors would get their money

225
00:08:44,536 --> 00:08:44,863
back.

226
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:47,644
Decision Desk HQ, for its part, said

227
00:08:47,644 --> 00:08:50,908
removing its seventeen fake polls didn't change

228
00:08:50,908 --> 00:08:53,547
its Senate and House forecasts at all.

229
00:08:53,827 --> 00:08:56,678
Michael Traugott, a former president of the

230
00:08:56,678 --> 00:09:00,191
polling researchers' association, called two cases in

231
00:09:00,191 --> 00:09:02,378
such a short time very troubling.

232
00:09:02,658 --> 00:09:04,804
So one side stresses the media.

233
00:09:04,804 --> 00:09:07,019
Another stresses the fraudsters.

234
00:09:07,019 --> 00:09:08,957
Everyone stresses the polls.

235
00:09:08,957 --> 00:09:10,618
We just count the facts.

236
00:09:10,898 --> 00:09:13,226
The midterms are on November third.

237
00:09:13,226 --> 00:09:15,355
You'll see a lot of polls before

238
00:09:15,355 --> 00:09:18,148
then. So here's Linda's practical guide to

239
00:09:18,148 --> 00:09:19,479
not getting Kevined.

240
00:09:19,759 --> 00:09:22,112
Rule one: never trust one poll.

241
00:09:22,112 --> 00:09:24,238
Look at the average of many.

242
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:26,591
One wild poll is usually just a

243
00:09:26,591 --> 00:09:27,351
wild poll.

244
00:09:27,631 --> 00:09:29,318
Rule two: who did the poll?

245
00:09:29,318 --> 00:09:30,756
Have you heard of them?

246
00:09:30,756 --> 00:09:32,757
Do they have a track record from

247
00:09:32,757 --> 00:09:33,694
past elections?

248
00:09:33,975 --> 00:09:36,129
Rule three: does it say how many

249
00:09:36,129 --> 00:09:38,688
people, how they were reached, and the

250
00:09:38,688 --> 00:09:39,765
margin of error?

251
00:09:39,765 --> 00:09:41,179
No details, no trust.

252
00:09:41,459 --> 00:09:43,723
Rule four: if a poll is exactly

253
00:09:43,723 --> 00:09:46,134
what your side was hoping for, be

254
00:09:46,134 --> 00:09:49,274
extra suspicious. That goes for every side.

255
00:09:49,554 --> 00:09:52,475
Rule five: remember that even honest polls

256
00:09:52,475 --> 00:09:55,465
can miss. Polls struggle to measure turnout

257
00:09:55,465 --> 00:09:58,177
and enthusiasm, and in tight races they

258
00:09:58,177 --> 00:09:59,985
can pick the wrong winner.

259
00:10:00,265 --> 00:10:02,734
Polls are a weather forecast, not the

260
00:10:02,734 --> 00:10:05,203
weather. The only poll that counts is

261
00:10:05,203 --> 00:10:06,804
the one on election day.

262
00:10:07,084 --> 00:10:10,206
Recap. A firm called Wedgewood Polls admitted

263
00:10:10,206 --> 00:10:12,981
on October twentieth that almost all its

264
00:10:12,981 --> 00:10:15,062
election surveys were made up.

265
00:10:15,342 --> 00:10:17,702
Its numbers reached the New York Times

266
00:10:17,702 --> 00:10:20,371
and forecast sites, which have pulled them,

267
00:10:20,371 --> 00:10:22,916
and one Senate candidate says they helped

268
00:10:22,916 --> 00:10:23,972
end her campaign.

269
00:10:24,252 --> 00:10:26,951
Both parties' officials are angry, just at

270
00:10:26,951 --> 00:10:30,036
different people. And everyone is checking their

271
00:10:30,036 --> 00:10:31,771
polls a lot more carefully.

272
00:10:32,051 --> 00:10:34,605
Back at the stand, Kevin has updated

273
00:10:34,605 --> 00:10:37,655
his sign. It now says: ninety-seven percent

274
00:10:37,655 --> 00:10:39,357
approval. Source: Kevin.

275
00:10:39,357 --> 00:10:40,705
